# Chronicle — Continuation Prompt

Paste this entire file at the start of a new Claude Code session to pick up where we left off.

---

## Project

**Chronicle** — a personal diary/calendar PWA. Vanilla JS (ES modules), HTML5, CSS3. No framework, no build step. Runs directly in browser.

**Location:** `s:\Coding\Diary Calendar\`

**File structure:**
```
index.html
js/
  app.js        — main orchestrator: state, rendering, modals, navigation
  calendar.js   — date math helpers
  diary.js      — diary contenteditable, autosave, format toolbar singleton
  events.js     — add/update/delete/toggleTodo helpers
  gestures.js   — long-press via touch events (swipe navigation removed)
  sync.js       — dirty-flag / Google Drive stub
  undo.js       — undo/redo stack
css/
  base.css          — CSS variables, reset, typography
  ribbon.css        — top header/ribbon buttons
  week-view.css     — week grid layout (Mon-start vs Sun-start)
  day-card.css      — individual day cards, diary area, format toolbar
  expanded-day.css  — full-screen day overlay
  modals.css        — bottom sheets, settings dropdown, date picker
  agenda.css        — agenda/events list panel
  responsive.css    — max-width 480px centering, desktop border
assets/
  icons.svg     — SVG sprite
```

---

## What Is Complete (Phase 1)

- Week grid: Mon-start (Sat+Sun half-height in weekend column) and Sun-start layouts
- Day cards: lined diary area (fixed height, internal scroll), events strip, todo checkboxes
- **Navigation: edge buttons only** — top/bottom arrows = prev/next week, left/right side arrows = prev/next month
- Long-press on any day card → Quick Actions sheet (add event/todo/reminder, expand, copy, clear)
  - Uses touch events (`touchstart`/`touchmove`/`touchend`) for Android reliability
  - 12px movement threshold cancels long-press to avoid mis-fires
  - `_suppressNextCardClick` flag eats the trailing click Android emits after a long-press
- Tap on day card (anywhere except diary area) → opens full-screen expanded day overlay
- Expanded day: diary + events list with edit/delete/check, "+ Add item" button
- Format toolbar: Bold, Italic, Underline, Highlight, Text Colour
  - Text colour: shows 5 recently used colours + a conic-gradient `<input type="color">` swatch
    for custom colours. Recent list seeded with defaults, updates as user picks colours.
  - Custom colour picker opens at a vivid red (`#FF0000`) if last colour was dark/desaturated
  - Highlight: 6 fixed swatches (yellow, green, blue, pink, orange, purple)
  - Compound buttons: tap = apply last colour, long-press/arrow = colour picker popup
  - Toolbar constrained to 480px app width on desktop via `left/right: max(0px, calc(50vw - 240px))`
  - Positioned above keyboard via `window.visualViewport`
  - When visible on desktop, `weekContainer` gets class `toolbar-open` adding `padding-bottom: 44px`
    so the bottom nav arrow isn't covered
- Add-event modal (bottom sheet): type segmented control, date, time, reminder, repeat, notes
- Quick Actions → Add Event/Todo/Reminder flow
- Settings dropdown: theme toggle, week-start toggle, FY week numbering, export/import JSON
- Agenda panel: filterable list of all events/todos/reminders with inline detail + edit/delete
- Date picker modal
- Back button from ALL modals returns to week view (never exits app):
  - Every modal calls `history.pushState` on open
  - Every close button / backdrop calls `history.back()`
  - `popstate` handler closes whichever overlay is open
- Undo/redo (Ctrl+Z / Ctrl+Y)
- Dark mode
- Midnight auto-refresh
- Data stored in `localStorage` key `chronicle_data`

---

## Known Remaining Issues

- **Format toolbar long-press on compound buttons (mobile):** The `_compoundApply` function uses
  pointer events (`pointerdown`/`pointerup`) for the tap-vs-hold distinction. This works on desktop
  and most mobile browsers, but may still be unreliable on some Android browsers. If reported,
  consider replacing with the same touchstart/touchend approach used in gestures.js.
- **Diary area touch-action:** The diary area has `pointerdown` with `e.stopPropagation()` which
  is harmless now (gestures use touch events), but could be cleaned up.

